When does the computational overhead of nonlinear model predictive control (NMPC) pay off in an exothermic reactor? This question is answered for a three-state continuous stirred tank reactor with jacket dynamics and a cooling valve limited in amplitude and slew rate. NMPC is compared only with robustly tuned PID baselines, AMIGO and an ITAE-optimal PID constrained to a maximum sensitivity of 1.6, all acting through the same valve with a rate-aware anti-windup. The NMPC uses a CasADi/IPOPT formulation with an RK4 prediction model and is evaluated with true-state access (Oracle) and with an augmented extended Kalman filter and moving-horizon estimator that reconstructs concentration and a fouling factor from two thermocouples. Near the design point the NMPC lowers the servo IAE of the best PID by only 2.3\%. After an abrupt loss of heat transfer to 70\% it reduces the IAE of unfiltered AMIGO by 21.0\% (13.5\% against AMIGO with a measurement filter), and the output-feedback variant stays within 1.7\% of the Oracle; a decomposition attributes 25\% of the gain to online adaptation of the fouling factor, which a gain-scheduled PID also captures, and 77\% to planning against the valve rate limit. Valve travel is 15--24\% lower than for unfiltered PID loops but not lower than for filtered ones. Without an integrating disturbance model both NMPC variants keep an offset of about 9~K under unmeasured feed disturbances, independent of the horizon; with one the offset is below 0.05~K. Below a critical heat transfer of 63.75\% no controller can reach the setpoint at full cooling. Each NMPC sample costs 31--39~ms against 0.09~ms for PID. Five criteria summarize when nonlinear prediction is worth its cost.
